1. High-level purpose

Goal:A fully programmable simulation engine that:

models octopus‑style distributed intelligence (brain + arms + micro‑nodes)

runs long‑baseline experiments in complex environments

encrypts all telemetry, behaviors, and lineages under your control

supports both interactive and passive‑alert modes

Use cases: AI research, robotics, space probes, emergent behavior studies, advanced game/sim AI.

2. Core architecture

2.1 Cognition stack

Intent Core (Global Brain):

Defines mission goals, reward functions, constraints.

Receives summarized feedback, not raw sensor streams.

Can be swapped: rule‑based, RL, or hybrid.

Arm Controllers (Semi‑Autonomous Agents):

Each arm is its own agent with:

local state

local memory

local policy loop

Can act without central approval.

Can coordinate with neighboring arms via local messages.

Micro‑Nodes (Suckers):

Tiny sensor/actuator units.

Provide tactile, chemical, proximity, and contact events.

Run ultra‑simple rules (reflexes, thresholds, triggers).

Communication Spine (Segmented Bus):

Ladder‑style message bus (micro‑node → arm → core).

Local chatter stays local; only escalated signals reach the core.

Reduces bandwidth, increases autonomy and resilience.

3. World simulation layer

Physics engine:

Soft‑body deformation for arms and body.

Fluid or medium simulation (water, gas, abstract fields).

Collision and constraint solver.

Environment model:

Spatial grid or continuous space.

Fields: light, chemical gradients, obstacles, entities.

External agents: prey, threats, artifacts, “probes”.

Scenario system:

JSON/YAML mission configs.

Define environment, objectives, time horizon, logging level.

4. Data, telemetry & lineages

State streams:

Per‑step snapshots of:

Intent Core state

each arm’s state

micro‑node aggregates

environment state

Event log:

Discrete events: grasp, explore, avoid, cooperate, discover, fail.

Append‑only, time‑ordered.

Behavioral lineages:

Cluster runs into “strategies” (e.g., cautious explorer, aggressive hunter).

Assign lineage IDs and track evolution over time.

Store lineage metadata separately from raw logs.

5. Encryption & security model

Master key & identity:

Master key derived from your passphrase using Argon2/PBKDF2.

All long‑term storage is encrypted at rest.

Encrypted telemetry vault:

Raw logs, state streams, and replays stored with AES‑256.

Indexes (lineage IDs, run IDs) stored in a compact encrypted catalog.

Controlled plaintext export:

boe export --view <filter> --format csv|json

Only exports filtered, aggregated views (never full raw vault by default).

Every export session is logged and signed.

Signature & integrity:

Optional signing of runs and exports with a personal keypair.

Tamper‑evident logs.

6. Runtime & orchestration

Simulation engine:

Step‑based or continuous time.

Supports batch runs and long‑baseline missions.

Configurable speed (real‑time, accelerated, headless).

Scheduler:

Queue of missions with priorities.

Can run periodic sweeps (e.g., nightly experiments).

Tracks success/failure, resource usage, and lineage yield.

Passive‑Alert Mode:

Engine runs unattended.

When a new lineage crosses an “interesting” threshold, it:

writes encrypted logs

generates a summary

pushes a console notification or webhook.

7. Cockpit console

Views:

Live run monitor (arms, environment, events).

Lineage tree browser.

Performance & health dashboard.

Export & replay manager.

Modes:

Active: tweak missions, parameters, environment in near real‑time.

Passive‑Alert: console quiet until something significant happens.

Forensic: decrypt and replay past runs step‑by‑step.

CLI commands (examples):

boe run mission.yaml

boe status

boe lineage list

boe replay <run_id>

boe export --lineage <id> --format csv

